  "live_records": {
    "q-38761": {
      "dom_id": "step-q38761",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,C=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$",
      "worked_solution": "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14163453/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -5 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 11 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 7-2\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -18 \\\\ 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
