      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$",
      "worked_solution": "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14163453/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
